**Step‑by‑step analysis**

The six friends are  

\[
\{ \text{Amy},\text{Bob},\text{Carl},\text{Dave},\text{Elise},\text{Frank}\}
\]

and the ordered pairs \((x,y)\) that belong to the relation  

\[
R=\{\langle x,y\rangle : x \text{ has beaten } y\}
\]

are exactly the results listed:

\[
\begin{aligned}
R=&\{\langle\text{Amy},\text{Bob}\rangle,\;
      \langle\text{Bob},\text{Carl}\rangle,\;
      \langle\text{Frank},\text{Bob}\rangle,\\[2pt]
   &\langle\text{Amy},\text{Elise}\rangle,\;
      \langle\text{Carl},\text{Dave}\rangle,\;
      \langle\text{Elise},\text{Carl}\rangle,\\[2pt]
   &\langle\text{Elise},\text{Dave}\rangle,\;
      \langle\text{Frank},\text{Elise}\rangle,\;
      \langle\text{Frank},\text{Amy}\rangle\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Now test each property.

| Property | Definition | Does \(R\) satisfy it? | Reason |
|----------|------------|------------------------|--------|
| **Reflexive** | \(\forall x\;(xRx)\) | **No** | No pair \(\langle x,x\rangle\) occurs (a player never beats himself). |
| **Irreflexive** | \(\forall x\;\neg(xRx)\) | **Yes** | As above, every ordered pair has distinct players. |
| **Symmetric** | \(\forall x\forall y\;(xRy\Rightarrow yRx)\) | **No** | E.g. \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Bob}\rangle\in R\) but \(\langle\text{Bob},\text{Amy}\rangle\notin R\). |
| **Asymmetric** | \(\forall x\forall y\;(xRy\Rightarrow \neg(yRx))\) | **Yes** | Whenever \(\langle x,y\rangle\in R\) the reverse pair is absent; no pair and its converse both belong to \(R\). |
| **Antisymmetric** | \(\forall x\forall y\;(xRy\land yRx\Rightarrow x=y)\) | **Yes** | There is no pair of distinct players with both directions; the only case where both directions would hold is the impossible \(x=y\). |
| **Transitive** | \(\forall x\forall y\forall z\;(xRy\land yRz\Rightarrow xRz)\) | **No** | Counter‑example: \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Bob}\rangle\in R,\; \langle\text{Bob},\text{Carl}\rangle\in R\) but \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Carl}\rangle\notin R\). |

Thus  

- \(R\) is **irreflexive** and **asymmetric**;  
- it is **not reflexive**, **not symmetric**, **not transitive**, and **vacuously antisymmetric** (the only way to violate antisymmetry would be a mutual beating, which never occurs).

---

\[
\boxed{\text{Irreflexive, Asymmetric, (vacuously) Antisymmetric; not Reflexive, not Symmetric, not Transitive}}
\]

