### (b)  Properties of \(R\)

| Property | Explanation | Yes / No |
|----------|-------------|----------|
| **Reflexive** | Would require \(\langle x,x\rangle\in R\) for every \(x\). No statement says a person beats himself. | **No** |
| **Irreflexive** | Requires \(\langle x,x\rangle\notin R\) for all \(x\). Since none of the nine pairs has the same name on both sides, this holds. | **Yes** |
| **Symmetric** | Would need that whenever \(\langle x,y\rangle\in R\) then \(\langle y,x\rangle\in R\). The only pairs that are “inverse” are \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Bob}\rangle\) and \(\langle\text{Bob},\text{Amy}\rangle\) (the latter is **not** in \(R\)), and similarly for \(\langle\text{Frank},\text{Amy}\rangle\) and \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Frank}\rangle\). Hence symmetry fails. | **No** |
| **Asymmetric** | Requires: (i) \(\langle x,y\rangle\in R \Rightarrow \langle y,x\rangle\notin R\) (i.e., no two opposite pairs) and (ii) \(\langle x,x\rangle\notin R\) (irreflexive). From the list we see that no opposite pair appears, and there are no self‑pairs. Thus the condition holds. | **Yes** |
| **Antisymmetric** | Requires: \(\langle x,y\rangle\in R\) and \(\langle y,x\rangle\in R\) ⇒ \(x=y\). Because the relation is already asymmetric (no opposite pairs exist), this condition is automatically satisfied. | **Yes** |
| **Transitive** | Would need: if \(\langle x,y\rangle\in R\) and \(\langle y,z\rangle\in R\) then \(\langle x,z\rangle\in R\). Counter‑example: \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Bob}\rangle\in R\) and \(\langle\text{Bob},\text{Carl}\rangle\in R\) but \(\langle\text{Amy},\text{Carl}\rangle\notin R\). Hence transitivity fails. | **No** |

